 "is_vocab": false,
 "stimulus_html": "<p>“A word about TV: If a television is on, an infant will stare at it. This is not a sign of advanced development. TV entertains at a cost. Young children easily become dependent on the TV for stimulation and lose some of their natural drive to explore. A child with a plastic cup and spoon, a few wooden blocks, and a board book can think up fifty creative ways to use those objects; a child in front of a TV can only do one thing. . . .</p>\n<p>Another subtle but worrisome effect television has on its viewers is its tendency to promote passivity and a lack of creativity. Watching television requires little mental activity on the viewer’s part. You simply sit and let the images flow by. Some research suggests that this sort of nonparticipatory viewing fosters a short attention span, making it hard for children to apply themselves in school.”</p>\n<p align=\"right\">Benjamin Spock, <i>Dr. Spock’s Baby and Child Care</i>, 9th Edition © 2012 (Gallery Books)</p>\n<p><i>Refer to the excerpt provided.</i></p>",
